# Production resource overrides that differ from docker-compose.yml's shared defaults.
#
# Unlike staging.sample.env, this is not a complete override of every resource variable -
# production's containers currently set none of them at all (P114: they predate the cpu_shares
# keys entirely, so they sit at the kernel's default weight of 1024). Fixing that is a separate,
# larger change - recreating every production container from the current compose file - tracked
# by P114 itself. This file exists only for what P125 asked for: more app-tier CPU.
#
# capacity-content (2026-09-15) found the app container pegged at the shared CPU_LIMIT__APP
# default of 2 cores under 500+ concurrent users - 84.41% throttled at 1,000 - after the query-
# level fixes had already brought Postgres CPU down to 0.50-0.63 mean cores. The bottleneck is
# the app container's own CPU allocation, uniformly across every endpoint, not any one query.
# Production has cores the shared host's other stacks don't, so only production's app tier gets
# more - raising the shared default instead would hand staging and every local checkout the same
# increase and let them compete with production for it, which is exactly what P114 exists to stop.
#
# Copy into production's .env on damballa (or add to it) and recreate urbanlens_production_app -
# a running container's --cpus is fixed at creation, per Docker, so editing .env alone does
# nothing until the container is recreated.
#
# test_production_app_cpu_allocation.py fails if this drops to or below the shared default, or if
# another environment's app-tier limit rises to meet it.
#
# Not yet re-measured against tests/perf/k6/population.js - see P125's "what this does not
# establish" section. This raises the ceiling capacity-content found; it does not confirm what
# the new ceiling is.

CPU_LIMIT__APP=4
